Fire the cursor-pos callback on cursor-mode transitions

glfwSetInputMode(GLFW_CURSOR, ...) re-reads the OS cursor position into
window->virtualCursorPos before the platform performs its enter/exit
warps, and without firing the cursor position callback. Applications
that track the cursor position from callbacks (ImGui among them) desync
by the drift accumulated during GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED and observe it
replayed as one giant delta on the next motion event — the long-standing
"cursor jump" of glfw/glfw#2523 / #1523.

Apply the platform mode change first (it warps to the window center on
capture and back to the restore position on release), then report the
settled position through _glfwInputCursorPos so the callback fires and
consumers resync. Ordering matters: firing the callback before the
platform warp still leaves the restore-warp discontinuity at release.

Contract change: a cursor-pos callback may now arrive synchronously
inside glfwSetInputMode; apps should treat it as a resync, not motion.
Verified with repro/cursor_jump_repro.c (repo usdtweak-jumpingcursorv2).

--- a/src/input.c	2026-07-01 21:59:35
+++ b/src/input.c	2026-07-01 22:00:00
@@ -609,10 +609,22 @@
 
             window->cursorMode = value;
 
-            _glfw.platform.getCursorPos(window,
-                                        &window->virtualCursorPosX,
-                                        &window->virtualCursorPosY);
+            // usdtweak patch: apply the mode first (the platform performs its
+            // enter/exit cursor warps), then report the settled position
+            // through _glfwInputCursorPos so the cursor position callback
+            // fires and event-driven consumers (e.g. ImGui) stay in sync.
+            // Upstream re-reads the OS cursor position into virtualCursorPos
+            // before the warps and without firing the callback; apps tracking
+            // the position from callbacks desync by the accumulated capture
+            // drift and see it replayed as one giant delta on the next motion
+            // event (glfw/glfw#2523).
             _glfw.platform.setCursorMode(window, value);
+
+            {
+                double xpos, ypos;
+                _glfw.platform.getCursorPos(window, &xpos, &ypos);
+                _glfwInputCursorPos(window, xpos, ypos);
+            }
             return;
         }
 
